Q: Are JPMS modifiers `static` and `transitive` incompatible? A: <ul>
<li><p><code>requires static</code> is a compile time dependency, e.g. Lombok or API's like JPA, SLF4J, ....</p>
</li>
<li><p><code>requires transitive</code> automatically transfers this dependency to clients of the module. This is especially useful when the module exposes classes of the dependency to clients.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>For example, suppose we have modules <code>client</code> and <code>service</code>, and <code>client</code> &gt; <code>service</code> &gt; <code>thirdpartylib</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>module client {
    requires service; // automatically requires thirdpartylib too, see below
}
</code></pre>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>module service {
    exports com.service.some.api; // package level export

    requires transitive thirdpartylib; // transfers requires to client(s)
    requires static lombok; // only needed at compile time.
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now <code>service</code> contains this method:</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public ThirdPartyClass doSomething() {
   //....
   return ThirdPartyClass.of(/*...*/);
}
</code></pre>
<p>.. and <code>client</code> calls it, thereby reusing and in need of <code>ThirdPartyClass</code>. Since client gets the <code>transitive</code> module <code>thirdpartylib</code>  from service, it works automatically: client doesn't need to <code>require thirdpartylib</code> explicitly.</p>
<p>Combining <code>static</code> with <code>transitive</code> compiles, but is not very useful. Here, making lombok <code>static transitive</code> would transfer that <em>compile time</em> dependency from service to <code>client</code>. If <code>client</code> needs it too, at <em>compile</em> time, it doesn't need to <code>require</code> it. But it's good practice when you directly depend on a module because you use it in your own code, you explicitly list it (in <code>pom.xml</code> and/or <code>module-info.java</code> ).</p>

Q: Is it possible to start each line of an inline element with a given character? A: <h3>With a Pseudo-element</h3>
<p><em>For solution not using pseudo skip to to the next example.</em></p>
<p>You can, sort of, but it's messy. The best approach I can think (that is CSS-alone) is to use a pseudo element, and multiple text-shadows. One down-side to this is that it assumes the opening <code>&quot;</code> is always on the first line of the blockquote, so to get the quotes start at the <em>second</em> line, you'd need a text-indent, padding, and position the pseudo one line down:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>blockquote {
  overflow: hidden;
  line-height: 1.6;
  position: relative;
  padding-left: 1ch;
  text-indent: -1ch;
}

blockquote::before {
  content: "\22";
  position: absolute;
  top: 1lh;
  left: 1ch;
  text-shadow:
    0 1lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 2lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 3lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 4lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 5lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 6lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 7lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 8lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 9lh 0 currentcolor;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;blockquote&gt;Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipisicing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse" cillum dolore eu fugiat nulla pariatur.&lt;/blockquote&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>This approach leverages modern (2023)  <code>lh</code> unit so we don't have to calculate each offset.  &quot;Stamping&quot; with <code>text-shadow</code> works outside the actual (pseudo-)element boundary, but we prevent over-expanding with <code>overflow-hidden</code> on the blockquote.</p>
<p>You can play with it a bit further here: <a href="http://jsfiddle.net/nx296yc8/1/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">http://jsfiddle.net/nx296yc8/1/</a></p>
<p><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/DliSB.png" alt="enter image description here" /></p>
<h3>Without a Pseudo-element</h3>
<p>You could inline all of this if you're willing to forego the pseudo-element:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;blockquote style="
  overflow: hidden;
  line-height: 1.8;
  position: relative;
  padding-left: 1ch;
  text-indent: -1ch;"
&gt;&lt;span style="
  position: absolute;
  top: 1lh;
  left: 1ch;
  text-shadow:
    0 1lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 2lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 3lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 4lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 5lh 0 currentcolor,
    0 6lh 0 currentcolor"
&gt;&amp;quot;&lt;/span&gt; Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipisicing elit, sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse" cillum dolore eu fugiat nulla pariatur.
&lt;/blockquote&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
